::CW_U06_L07_Q01::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which action best begins integrated portfolio preparation?{
=A. List required parts and mark each present, missing, or needing revision.#Correct. This gives a clear readiness map. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Decorate the portfolio before checking contents.#Incorrect. First check completeness and purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Submit the first file found.#Incorrect. Selection should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Remove the author statement.#Incorrect. Reflection is part of portfolio synthesis. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q02::[MLA.CW.COM.01] Which piece belongs in a final portfolio?{
~A. A piece with no title and no revision.#Incorrect. This is not reader-ready. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A favorite piece the writer cannot explain.#Incorrect. Preference alone is weak evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. A revised piece that shows a specific craft skill and reader effect.#Correct. This supports portfolio evidence. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. A draft with comments still visible.#Incorrect. Draft comments weaken final readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q03::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] A portfolio statement discusses voice. Which evidence best matches it?{
~A. A random image pasted beside the title.#Incorrect. This does not show voice development. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A file name with no writing attached.#Incorrect. A file name is not craft evidence.
~C. A paragraph saying the writer worked hard.#Incorrect. Effort alone does not show voice. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. A revised story excerpt where dialogue and sentence rhythm reveal character voice.#Correct. This directly supports the stated craft growth.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q04::[MLA.CW.COM.01] Which portfolio order has the clearest purpose?{
~A. Alphabetical by file name with no explanation.#Incorrect. This may not guide craft understanding. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Story first for revised dialogue, poem second for voice through rhythm, statement last to explain growth.#Correct. This order guides the reader through evidence.
~C. Random order based on download time.#Incorrect. Random order does not support readers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Longest to shortest only because length matters most.#Incorrect. Length is not the main purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q05::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which note is most useful during a readiness pass?{
~A. Fix it later.#Incorrect. This is too vague. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Maybe change something.#Incorrect. This does not identify the problem. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Preview script and make character labels consistent.#Correct. This names a specific publication-readiness action.
~D. It looks fine from far away.#Incorrect. Readiness requires specific checks. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q06::[MLA.CW.COM.01] How should an author statement connect to selected pieces?{
=A. It should name skills the reader can see in the portfolio.#Correct. The statement and evidence should support each other.
~B. It should discuss unrelated goals only.#Incorrect. The statement should connect to the work.
~C. It should hide all revision evidence.#Incorrect. Revision evidence supports growth. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. It should replace the creative pieces.#Incorrect. It explains the pieces; it does not replace them.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q07::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which presentation choice best supports the portfolio?{
~A. Read the longest excerpt regardless of purpose.#Incorrect. Excerpt choice should be focused. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Avoid explaining the excerpt.#Incorrect. The presentation needs craft reasoning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Summarize every assignment from the course.#Incorrect. That is too broad. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Choose one excerpt that shows a craft choice connected to portfolio growth.#Correct. This connects presentation and portfolio evidence. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q08::[MLA.CW.COM.01] Which problem should be fixed before final publication?{
~A. The writer can explain the piece clearly.#Incorrect. That is a strength, not a problem.
=B. The final file includes bracketed notes that say add ending later.#Correct. Draft notes show the piece is not final.
~C. The portfolio has a clear title.#Incorrect. A clear title supports readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The author statement names revision growth.#Incorrect. That supports synthesis. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q09::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] What makes a portfolio integrated?{
~A. The files are unrelated but colorful.#Incorrect. Visual decoration does not create integration. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The portfolio has no explanation.#Incorrect. Author reasoning is needed. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. The selected pieces, statement, revision evidence, and presentation plan connect to shared growth.#Correct. Integration means the parts work together. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. The presentation ignores the pieces.#Incorrect. The presentation should connect to the portfolio.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q10::[MLA.CW.COM.01] Which answer best explains reader effect?{
~A. I put this first because I found it first.#Incorrect. This does not explain reader effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. I added random spacing.#Incorrect. Random spacing does not support readers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I like the color.#Incorrect. Preference is not reader effect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. I revised the line breaks so the reader pauses before the speaker's decision.#Correct. This names a choice and effect. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q11::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] A student marks "needs revision" beside the presentation plan. What should come next?{
=A. Write the exact missing action, such as add listening focus.#Correct. Specific actions guide revision. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Ignore the note.#Incorrect. The issue should be resolved. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Delete the portfolio.#Incorrect. One issue does not require deleting everything.
~D. Submit without checking.#Incorrect. Readiness requires follow-through. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q12::[MLA.CW.COM.01] Which choice shows revision evidence?{
~A. A blank page.#Incorrect. There is no evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A final piece with no explanation and no draft trail.#Incorrect. This may not show revision evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. A draft and final version with a note explaining how feedback improved dialogue.#Correct. This shows change and reasoning. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. A decoration folder.#Incorrect. Decoration is not revision evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q13::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which author rationale is strongest?{
~A. I included this because it was easy.#Incorrect. Ease is not a strong rationale. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. I included this poem because revising the image pattern made the speaker's change clearer.#Correct. This connects selection, revision, and effect. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. I included this because it is first alphabetically.#Incorrect. File order is not craft rationale. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I included this because I needed another page.#Incorrect. Filler is not portfolio evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q14::[MLA.CW.COM.01] Which statement describes publication formatting?{
~A. Changing the genre completely.#Incorrect. Formatting prepares the genre; it does not automatically change it.
~B. Avoiding all revision.#Incorrect. Formatting and revision can both matter. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Refusing to preview the file.#Incorrect. Previewing supports readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Preparing the piece with title, spacing, labels, conventions, and file readiness.#Correct. These are reader-facing format concerns. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q15::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] What should a student do if the portfolio order feels random?{
=A. Create a reader-focused order and explain the reason.#Correct. Purposeful order helps the reader follow the portfolio.
~B. Leave it random.#Incorrect. Random order weakens integration. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Remove every piece.#Incorrect. Reordering is the better fix. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Add unrelated colors.#Incorrect. Colors do not solve organization. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q16::[MLA.CW.COM.01] Which checkpoint response shows mastery?{
~A. I am done because I clicked submit.#Incorrect. Submission alone does not show mastery. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. I chose whatever was easiest.#Incorrect. Selection should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. My portfolio order moves from revised story to poem to statement so readers see voice growth across genres.#Correct. This explains order and craft growth. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. I cannot explain the order.#Incorrect. Mastery includes rationale. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q17::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which final presentation note is strongest?{
~A. Listen to everything.#Incorrect. This is too broad. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Listen for how the clipped dialogue creates tension after the narrator avoids the question.#Correct. This gives a clear listening focus. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. I do not know what to listen for.#Incorrect. The presenter needs focus. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Ignore the excerpt.#Incorrect. The excerpt is central. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q18::[MLA.CW.COM.01] Which issue is a portfolio completeness problem?{
~A. The title is clear.#Incorrect. That supports readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The presentation has a listening focus.#Incorrect. That is a strength. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. The revised piece has paragraph breaks.#Incorrect. That supports readability. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. The required author statement is missing.#Correct. A missing required part must be resolved.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q19::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Why should the student avoid vague notes like "fix portfolio"?{
=A. They do not identify the exact action needed.#Correct. Specific notes make revision possible. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. They are too detailed.#Incorrect. They are not detailed enough. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. They replace the need for revision.#Incorrect. They do not guide revision. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. They prove mastery.#Incorrect. Mastery requires clear action and reasoning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q20::[MLA.CW.COM.01] Which answer best connects publication and presentation?{
~A. The presentation should ignore the portfolio.#Incorrect. They should connect. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Publication means no one reads the piece.#Incorrect. Publication prepares work for readers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. The portfolio shows the final work, and the presentation explains how selected craft choices work.#Correct. This captures the relationship between product and author reasoning.
~D. Presentation means only summary.#Incorrect. Presentation includes craft reasoning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q21::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which choice should be removed from a final portfolio file?{
~A. Clear title.#Incorrect. A clear title should remain. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Intentional line breaks.#Incorrect. Purposeful line breaks support poetry. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Author name if required.#Incorrect. Required author information should remain. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Unresolved comment saying check spelling.#Correct. Unresolved comments make the file look unfinished.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q22::[MLA.CW.COM.01] Which answer is strongest when explaining a selected excerpt?{
~A. I selected it randomly.#Incorrect. Selection should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. I selected it because it shows the exact revision I describe in my statement.#Correct. This connects excerpt and author reflection. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. I selected it because it is confusing.#Incorrect. Confusion does not support audience understanding. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I selected it but will not explain it.#Incorrect. Presentation requires explanation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q23::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which readiness pass checks genre-specific format?{
=A. Check poems for intentional line breaks and scripts for clear character labels.#Correct. Genre-specific formatting supports readers. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~B. Make every genre look identical.#Incorrect. Genres have different format needs. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Remove all titles.#Incorrect. Titles often prepare readers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Add random spacing.#Incorrect. Random spacing distracts. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q24::[MLA.CW.COM.01] A student says the statement and portfolio do not match. What is the best fix?{
~A. Hide the selected pieces.#Incorrect. The portfolio needs evidence. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Align the statement's claims with evidence in the selected pieces.#Correct. This creates a coherent portfolio. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Delete all reflection.#Incorrect. Reflection is part of the course. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Ignore the mismatch.#Incorrect. Mismatch weakens integration. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L07_Q25::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which final decision best shows Unit 6 integration?{
~A. Submit one rough draft only.#Incorrect. That is incomplete. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Submit a presentation with no portfolio.#Incorrect. The unit requires integrated work. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Submit publication-ready pieces, a connected author statement, revision evidence, and a presentation plan with craft focus.#Correct. This combines the unit's core skills.
~D. Submit unrelated files without explanation.#Incorrect. Integration requires purpose and connection. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}
